<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert of Rhuddlan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhuddlan">Rhuddlan</a> was a Norman knight, cousin and lieutenant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-davranches-earl-of-chester">Hugh d'Avranches, Earl of Chester</a>. From his castle at Rhuddlan he conquered much of the north Welsh coast and claimed rule over Gwynedd, after he and Hugh captured its king, Gruffudd ap Cynan. He was killed by Welsh raiders near the Great Orme, probably in 1093.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-of-rhuddlan.jpg" alt="Robert of Rhuddlan" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Peel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hauling_logs_near_Rhuddlan_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert was the son of Humphrey de Tillieul and Adeliza de Grentemesnil and a brother of Arnaud de Tilleul. He is recorded as a squire at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> and appears to have come to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welsh-marches">Welsh Marches</a> before 1066 in the king's service. His son, William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhuddlan">Rhuddlan</a>, was lost in the White Ship disaster in 1120.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Hugh d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a> became Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> in 1070, Robert appears to have been made his commander of troops in 1072. He at once made war on the Welsh, took land in the cantref of Tegeingl and built a motte-and-bailey castle at Twthill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhuddlan">Rhuddlan</a>, holding these lands as Earl Hugh's vassal. In 1075 he lent Norman troops to Gruffudd ap Cynan in his bid for the throne of Gwynedd against Trahaearn ap Caradog, and when Trahaearn drove Gruffudd to Ireland later that year, Robert used the civil war to seize the cantrefs of Rhos and Rhufoniog and build a castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deganwy">Deganwy</a>.</p><p>Trahaearn's death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mynydd-carn">Battle of Mynydd Carn</a> in 1081 removed the main check on his advance. Gruffudd was taken by treachery at Rhug near Corwen and held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a>, and Robert, who claimed his lands, built castles at Bangor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a> and Aberlleiniog. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> records him as holding all of North Wales, apart from the lands of the bishoprics of Bangor and St Asaph, directly of the king for an annual rent of £40. In 1088 he backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> and took part in the siege of Rochester, whose defenders had to surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a>, Robert was resting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deganwy">Deganwy</a> when news came that raiders in three ships had landed under the Great Orme. He hurried there and, seeing the tide about to float their ships before his men arrived, charged down the slope with only his armour-bearer. He was killed by javelins, and the raiders sailed off with his head on a mast. Earl Hugh took over his lands, most of which were lost in the Welsh revolt of 1094.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert of Rhuddlan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hugh and his cousin <b>Robert of Rhuddlan</b> advanced along the north Welsh coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-chester">County Palatine of Chester</a></li></ul></section>
